    if not neighbors or d(next,q)>=d(curr,q):
        return next
    else:
        return next

# Solution 
The algorithm for this task is a greedy search on the proximity graph G. Starting from a random node v in layer L0 (the root), we find the nearest neighbor of q within that layer by following a simple greedy search algorithm [46,47]. After finding the nearest neighbor viin layerLi, we continue this process, starting from viin layerLi-1. We proceed until reaching the bottom (layerL0). 

The pseudocode for this algorithm is provided in Algorithm 2 and a visual illustration is shown in Figure 1 (left). Answering k-Nearest Neighbors for k>1can be achieved by considering a set of candidates at each step in the greedy algorithm or running a beam search to consider multiple paths to the query [31,46].

The function GreedySearch takes two arguments: G is the proximity graph and q is the query point. It returns the vertex with minimum distance from q within layer Li+1. 

To ensure that we have an approximate nearest neighbor of q in X, we need to follow a simple greedy search algorithm on top of HENN graph. Starting from a random node v in the root (layerL0), we find the closest neighbor to q by considering all neighbors of current in G and selecting the one with minimum distance d(next,q). We repeat this process until we reach a local minimum where no closer neighbor exists within the given threshold distance d.

